---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: ""
bodyClass: home
description: "Astrophotography by Dustin K. — nebulae, galaxies, and the Sun captured through a telescope."
ogImage: /assets/img/og-home.jpg
# Gates the Currently-Imaging script in the base layout, the same way
# `galleryPage` gates gallery.js. Only this page carries the #now-imaging
# section, so only this page should pay for now-imaging.js.
homePage: true
---

{#
	Preload the hero background image — it's the LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)
	element, so getting it fetching early reduces perceived load time.
	`as="image"` tells the browser to assign image fetch priority instead of
	the default "low" priority a background-image would otherwise receive.

	Two MEDIA-SCOPED preloads so each viewport preloads only the crop it will
	actually paint, matching the CSS hero swap in main.css:
	  • desktop (≥769px)  → landscape crop hero-bg-veil-srgb.webp (the base rule)
	  • mobile  (≤768px)  → portrait "west" crop hero-bg-veil-west-srgb.webp,
	    which the `@media (max-width: 768px)` rule paints via !important.
	Before this split, EVERY device preloaded the desktop crop and mobile
	clients then downloaded the portrait crop the CSS actually renders — a
	wasted full-image fetch on the most bandwidth-constrained visitors. These
	use Media Queries Level 4 RANGE syntax (`width < 769px` / `width >= 769px`),
	a single complementary split at the 769px boundary. The CSS hero swap keys
	off `@media (max-width: 768px)`; `width < 769px` is the boundary-equivalent
	of `max-width: 768px` (both select ≤768px), so the preloaded file is always
	the rendered one. `media` on <link rel=preload> and range syntax are both
	honored by all evergreen browsers. The mobile crop already exists (generated
	alongside the desktop srgb webp on 2026-04-22); no new asset was needed.
#}
{% block head %}
<link rel="preload" as="image" href="/assets/img/hero-bg-veil-srgb.webp" type="image/webp" media="(width >= 769px)" />
<link rel="preload" as="image" href="/assets/img/hero-bg-veil-west-srgb.webp" type="image/webp" media="(width < 769px)" />
{% endblock %}

<!--
	HOME PAGE
	`publishedImages` comes from src/_data/publishedImages.js — a derived
	data file that filters out any entry in images.json marked
	`"published": false`. We use the filtered list (not the raw `images`
	global) so unpublished placeholders never appear in the Recent Work grid.
	`site` comes from src/_data/site.json.
-->

<!-- ===== HERO ===== -->
<section class="hero">

	<!--
		Background image — replace the gradient with your best astrophoto.
		The image is set as a CSS background-image. You can swap it to any
		of your gallery images. The gradient fallback ensures the page looks
		reasonable even before images load.
	-->
	<div class="hero-bg" style="background-image: url('/assets/img/hero-bg-veil-srgb.webp'); background-color: #09090f;"></div>

	<!-- Dark gradient overlay for text legibility -->
	<div class="hero-overlay"></div>

	<div class="hero-content">

		<!-- Large logo — split into styled spans so each part gets its own color -->
		<h1 class="hero-logo" aria-label="Dust In Space">
			<span class="logo-dust">DUST</span><span class="logo-dot">·</span><span class="logo-in">IN</span><span class="logo-dot">·</span><span class="logo-space">SPACE</span>
		</h1>

		<p class="hero-tagline">{{ site.tagline }}</p>

		<a href="/gallery/" class="hero-cta">Explore the Gallery</a>
	</div>

	<!-- Animated scroll indicator -->
	<div class="hero-scroll" aria-hidden="true">Scroll</div>

</section>

<!--
	NAV SLOT BORDER — desktop only, home page only.
	Positioned at exactly the nav bar's bottom edge. z-index: 201 puts it
	above the sliding hero title (z-index: 200) so it remains visible as
	the title slides through it — the "card through slot" visual.
	JS fades this out after the title settles in the nav bar.
-->
<div class="nav-slot-border" aria-hidden="true"></div>

{#
	CURRENTLY IMAGING — live card fed by now-imaging.js (spec:
	docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-01-currently-imaging-design.md).
	The section ships HIDDEN and stays hidden unless the script fetches a
	valid status.json from live.dustin.space. Without JS, or if the rig has
	never published, this markup renders nothing — by design there is never
	an "unavailable" card on the homepage (spec §6.2).

	Division of labour: every element that reports what the rig is DOING ships
	empty here and is filled by JS (#now-image, #now-frame-tag, #now-name,
	#now-designation, #now-caption). The surrounding chrome is static markup
	and is not JS's to write: the "Currently imaging" label, the "What's this?"
	button, and the whole explainer dialog below.
#}
<section class="now-imaging" id="now-imaging" hidden aria-labelledby="now-imaging-label">
	<div class="container">
		<div class="now-status">
			<span class="now-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
			<span class="now-label" id="now-imaging-label">Currently imaging</span>
		</div>
		<div class="now-card">
			{# aspect-ratio is set inline by JS from status.frame.width/height before
			   the image loads, so the card reserves its box and nothing shifts. #}
			<div class="now-frame" id="now-frame">
				<img id="now-image" alt="" decoding="async" loading="lazy" width="3" height="2" />
				<span class="now-frame-tag" id="now-frame-tag"></span>
			</div>
			<div class="now-meta">
				<h2 class="now-name" id="now-name"></h2>
				<p class="now-designation" id="now-designation"></p>
				<p class="now-caption" id="now-caption"></p>
				<button type="button" class="now-whats" id="now-whats" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-controls="now-dialog">What's this?</button>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	{#
		EXPLAINER — native <dialog>: showModal() gives focus trapping, Escape to
		close, and an ::backdrop for free, with no library. Content voice per
		project CLAUDE.md: astronomer + layperson at once. Historical claims are
		sourced in docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-01-currently-imaging.md
		(Task 9, "Sources"); keep copy and sources in sync when editing.

		Three wording decisions from that fact-check, recorded here so a future
		edit does not silently undo them:
		  • The plate paragraph describes a real physical technique and is
		    sourced to Malin 1988, "The Superimposition of Many Plates", whose
		    abstract reports superimposing film copies of 36 sky-limited plates
		    of one field. It still carries NO date claim: the copy says what was
		    done and who did it, not when the practice began.
		  • The Bayer sentence says "half or more of the light in any one
		    color". Not "three quarters", which is exact for red and blue but
		    wrong for green (half the pixels, not a quarter), and not "most",
		    which is wrong for green in the other direction.
		  • "Most finished images in the gallery are dozens of frames" is a
		    hedge with a reason: images.json has a Pleiades L-Pro variant built
		    from 8 frames, so "every" would be false against our own data.
	#}
	<dialog class="now-dialog" id="now-dialog" aria-labelledby="now-dialog-title">
		{#
			The close button is a SIBLING of the scrolling body, not a child of
			it, and that placement is load-bearing: it is absolutely positioned,
			so inside .now-dialog-body it would resolve against the scroll
			container and scroll off the top with the text. Probed at 1280px —
			scrolling the body 204px moved the button from y=218 to y=14, out of
			the dialog entirely. As a sibling it resolves against the dialog's
			own box (which showModal() makes position:fixed) and stays put.
		#}
		<button type="button" class="now-dialog-close" id="now-dialog-close" aria-label="Close">×</button>
		{#
			tabindex="-1" autofocus puts the initial focus on the BODY rather
			than on the close button above it, and that is a keyboard fix, not a
			preference. The body is the scroll container (the dialog itself is
			overflow: hidden so it does not grow a second scrollbar), and it
			contains no focusable children at all. With focus on the close
			button, whose own scroll ancestor is the non-scrolling dialog box,
			the arrow and Page keys have nothing to act on, so a keyboard user
			could not read past the first screen of the essay. Focusing the
			scroller itself makes those keys scroll it.
			tabindex="-1" keeps the body out of the Tab order, so Tab still
			cycles the close button alone. autofocus cannot fire on page load
			here: a closed <dialog> is display: none per the UA stylesheet (and
			the whole section ships hidden besides), and an unrendered element
			is not a focus candidate. It takes effect when showModal() runs its
			focusing steps.
		#}
		<div class="now-dialog-body" tabindex="-1" autofocus>
			<h2 id="now-dialog-title">What you're looking at</h2>

			<h3>Why this frame looks rough</h3>
			<p>This is a single exposure, usually a few minutes long, straight off the camera: grainy, gray, and faint. It has had nothing done to it except a quick stretch so you can see it at all. Most finished images in the gallery are dozens of frames like this one, sometimes hundreds, combined.</p>

			<h3>Stacking: from glass plates to pixel rejection</h3>
			<p>The word is literal. Astronomers working on glass plates would lay copies of several exposures of the same field one atop another, in register, and print through the stack: each exposure added a little more signal from the object and a little less of the random grain, and the stack reached fainter than any single plate could. David Malin made one such image at the Anglo-Australian Observatory from thirty-six plates of a single field. The technique outlived the glass, and the name came with it.</p>
			<p>Today the stack is arithmetic. Software aligns every frame star to star, then for each pixel it looks at the whole column of values across all the frames and takes a robust average. Anything that appears in only one frame, a satellite trail, a cosmic-ray hit, a hot pixel, is thrown out before averaging: that step is called pixel rejection. The faint glow of a nebula shows up in every frame, so it survives. The grain is random, so it averages away. Noise falls with the square root of the frame count, so forty frames are about twice as clean as ten. That is why a night's work is many short exposures rather than one long one.</p>

			<h3>Why black-and-white?</h3>
			<p>Because the camera is black-and-white on purpose. A color camera has a fixed mosaic of tiny red, green and blue filters over its pixels, so each pixel only ever sees one color: only a quarter of them are red, only a quarter are blue, and half or more of the light in any one color never lands on a pixel that can record it. A monochrome camera has no such mosaic, so every pixel collects the whole band, at full resolution. Color is added afterwards by shooting through filters one at a time: red, green and blue for stars and galaxies, or the narrow bands that nebulae actually glow in, hydrogen, oxygen and sulfur. Each single frame is one of those bands. What you see above is one band of light from one object, and the color image is built from stacks of frames like it.</p>
		</div>
	</dialog>
</section>

<!-- ===== RECENT WORK ===== -->
<section class="recent-work">
	<div class="container">

		<div class="section-header">
			<h2>Latest Captures</h2>
		</div>

		<!--
			Homepage preview grid — sorted newest-first.

			STRUCTURE: Each image is wrapped in .gallery-card-wrap rather than
			being a plain <a>. This wrapper lets us add a <button> as a sibling
			to the <a> link — nesting <button> inside <a> is invalid HTML, but
			siblings inside a shared <div> wrapper is perfectly fine.

			ROWS: 3-column grid. Items 1–6 (rows 1–2) are always visible.
			Items 7–15 (rows 3–5) carry .gallery-card-wrap--extra and are
			hidden by CSS until the "View more" button is clicked.

			FADE: .gallery-fade-overlay is a gradient over the grid that visually
			"cuts off" row 2, inviting the user to click "View more". JS removes
			it when the grid is expanded.
		-->
		<div class="gallery-preview-wrap">
			<div class="gallery-grid gallery-grid--home" id="home-gallery-grid">

				{% for image in publishedImages | sortByDate %}
				{% if loop.index <= 15 %}
				{# Resolve primary variant for thumbnail and metadata #}
				{% set pv = image.variants | primaryVariant %}

				<div class="gallery-card-wrap{% if loop.index > 6 %} gallery-card-wrap--extra{% endif %}">

					{#
						Card markup lives in partials/gallery-card.njk, shared with
						the gallery index. The include inherits image, pv, and loop
						from this scope. The .gallery-card-wrap wrapper stays here —
						it's home-only (hangs the View-More / hidden-row logic off a
						sibling of the card).
					#}
					{% include "partials/gallery-card.njk" %}

				</div><!-- /.gallery-card-wrap -->
				{% endif %}
				{% endfor %}

			</div><!-- /#home-gallery-grid -->

			<!--
				Gradient overlay that fades out the second row of cards.
				Always rendered — the fade frames the grid as a preview regardless
				of total image count. JS adds .hidden to dissolve it on expand.
			-->
			<div class="gallery-fade-overlay" id="gallery-fade-overlay" aria-hidden="true"></div>

		</div><!-- /.gallery-preview-wrap -->

		<!--
			"View more" button — always shown.
			Clicking it dissolves the fade, reveals extra cards (if any), and
			surfaces the "View All" link. JS adds .hidden after expansion.
		-->
		<div class="gallery-view-more-row" id="gallery-view-more-row">
			<button class="gallery-view-more-btn" id="gallery-view-more-btn">View More</button>
		</div>

		<!--
			"View All" link — hidden until View More is clicked.
		-->
		<div class="gallery-view-all-row" id="gallery-view-all-row">
			<a href="/gallery/" class="hero-cta">View All Images</a>
		</div>

	</div>
</section>

<!-- ===== INTRO BLURB ===== -->
<section class="home-intro">
	<div class="container">
		<div class="home-intro-inner">
			<h2>Light From Across the Universe</h2>
			<p>
				Every image here represents hours — sometimes dozens of hours — of photons
				collected from nebulae, galaxies, and our own Sun. From my backyard in the
				Pacific Northwest to remote observatories in New Mexico and Australia,
				I chase the deep sky with a camera, a telescope, and a lot of patience.
			</p>
			<p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
				Welcome to <strong>DUST·IN·SPACE</strong>.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<!--
	HOMEPAGE GALLERY — View More

	Clicking #gallery-view-more-btn adds .expanded to the grid container,
	which reveals .gallery-card-wrap--extra rows (3–5) via CSS specificity,
	dissolves the fade overlay, and shows the "View All" row.
-->
<script>
(function () {
	// ── View More ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	var viewMoreBtn = document.getElementById('gallery-view-more-btn');
	var viewMoreRow = document.getElementById('gallery-view-more-row');
	var viewAllRow  = document.getElementById('gallery-view-all-row');
	var fadeOverlay = document.getElementById('gallery-fade-overlay');

	if (viewMoreBtn) {
		viewMoreBtn.addEventListener('click', function () {
			// Add .expanded to the grid container — CSS rule
			// .gallery-grid--home.expanded .gallery-card-wrap--extra { display: block }
			// overrides the default display:none. Using classList + CSS specificity
			// instead of inline style.display because the class-level rule wins over
			// clearing an inline style (which was the previous broken approach).
			var grid = document.getElementById('home-gallery-grid');
			if (grid) grid.classList.add('expanded');
			// Dissolve the gradient fade overlay
			if (fadeOverlay) fadeOverlay.classList.add('hidden');
			// Hide the "View more" button
			if (viewMoreRow) viewMoreRow.classList.add('hidden');
			// Show the "View All" button at the bottom
			if (viewAllRow) viewAllRow.classList.add('visible');

			// Move keyboard focus to the first newly-revealed card. The button
			// the user just activated is now display:none (viewMoreRow.hidden),
			// so without this focus would fall back to <body> and a keyboard
			// user would lose their place. The .gallery-card-wrap--extra rows
			// were display:none until .expanded above; their first <a.gallery-card>
			// is the first thing uncovered. If there are no extra rows (≤6
			// images) fall back to the freshly-shown "View All" link so focus
			// still lands somewhere sensible rather than nowhere.
			var firstExtra = grid && grid.querySelector('.gallery-card-wrap--extra .gallery-card');
			if (firstExtra) {
				firstExtra.focus();
			} else if (viewAllRow) {
				var viewAllLink = viewAllRow.querySelector('a');
				if (viewAllLink) viewAllLink.focus();
			}
		});
	}

}());
</script>

<!--
	SCROLL-DRIVEN TITLE ANIMATION
	Only active on the home page (bodyClass: home).

	Two-phase animation:

	  PHASE 1 — Logo stays in normal document flow (scrolls with the page,
	  stays above the tagline). As the user scrolls, a CSS scale() transform
	  shrinks the logo visually without affecting its layout size, so the
	  tagline position never shifts. transform-origin is "top center" so
	  the logo shrinks downward from its top edge.

	  PHASE 2 — When the logo has naturally scrolled to the height of the
	  nav bar (meaning phase1End pixels have been scrolled), the logo switches
	  to position:fixed at the nav bar. Because the logo was already at that
	  viewport Y when the switch happens, there is no jump.

	  A spacer div is inserted at the Phase 1→2 transition so the content
	  below (tagline, CTA) doesn't jump when the logo leaves normal flow.

	  The animation runs on BOTH desktop and mobile (mobile re-enabled
	  2026-04-22). Two width thresholds appear below and are NOT on/off
	  switches for the animation:
	    • 1299px (NAV_COLLAPSE_MAX) — where the nav collapses to the hamburger;
	      only the menu-open title-snap handler is gated on it.
	    • 768px — a device-feel tuning threshold inside pickConfig(): it swaps
	      the ease curve (quadratic on mobile, cubic on desktop), not whether
	      the animation runs.
	  The one case that fully skips the animation is prefers-reduced-motion
	  (handled below), which lets the static nav logo show instead.
-->
<script>
(function () {
	var heroLogo   = document.querySelector('.hero-logo');
	var navLogo    = document.querySelector('.site-logo');
	var scrollHint = document.querySelector('.hero-scroll');
	var slotBorder = document.querySelector('.nav-slot-border');
	if (!heroLogo) return;

	// Widest viewport at which the nav has collapsed to the hamburger.
	// MUST stay in sync with the `@media (width < 1300px)` nav-collapse query
	// in main.css (range syntax since 2026-07-12; collapse applies below 1300,
	// so this gate is 1299) — the menu-open title-snap handler below is gated
	// on it. tests/nav-breakpoint.test.js guards this pairing against drift.
	// (Raised from 768 on 2026-06-10 when the nav split into flanking
	// clusters and the collapse point moved.)
	var NAV_COLLAPSE_MAX = 1299;

	// Respect the user's OS-level motion preference (Settings → Accessibility
	// → Reduce Motion on macOS, or equivalent on other platforms).
	// CSS already handles prefers-reduced-motion for its own transitions;
	// this check covers the JS-driven scroll animation that CSS can't reach.
	// When reduced motion is preferred, skip the animation entirely and
	// let the static nav logo show via its default CSS rules.
	if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return;

	// ── Per-device animation tuning ───────────────────────────────────────────
	// The animation runs on both desktop AND mobile (mobile re-enabled 2026-04-22).
	// These knobs shape how the scroll animation FEELS on each form factor
	// independently. Edit the values inside either branch below to tune.
	//
	//   easeFn            — curve mapping linear scroll progress (0–1) to
	//                        rendered progress. Cubic (t*t*t) stays near 0 for
	//                        most of the scroll and snaps sharply to 1 at the
	//                        end — "hold then slot" feel. Quadratic (t*t) is
	//                        gentler; linear (t) is fully proportional.
	//   phase1Multiplier  — scales the scroll threshold where the animation
	//                        completes. 1.0 = finishes exactly when the logo
	//                        would naturally reach the nav position. <1.0 =
	//                        finishes sooner (faster response); >1.0 = later
	//                        (longer runway — useful if mobile's shorter hero
	//                        makes the natural distance feel rushed).
	function pickConfig() {
		if (window.innerWidth <= 768) {
			return {
				// Mobile — starting values; iterate from real-device feel
				easeFn:           function (t) { return t * t; },   // quadratic — gentler than cubic
				phase1Multiplier: 1.0,                              // natural scroll runway
			};
		}
		return {
			// Desktop — preserves pre-mobile-enable behavior exactly
			easeFn:           function (t) { return t * t * t; },   // cubic ease-in
			phase1Multiplier: 1.0,
		};
	}
	var cfg = pickConfig();

	// ── Measure the logo's natural position before touching anything ──────────
	var rect          = heroLogo.getBoundingClientRect();
	var heroFontPx    = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(heroLogo).fontSize);
	// startTop: the logo's top edge in DOCUMENT coordinates (viewport Y + scrollY).
	// Adding window.scrollY matters when the page loads already scrolled — e.g.
	// a bfcache/back-forward restore or a reload at a scrolled position. rect.top
	// alone is viewport-relative, so at scrollY>0 it would understate startTop and
	// throw off phase1End. This matches the resize path's math (spRect.top + scrollY).
	var startTop      = rect.top + window.scrollY;
	var logoHeight    = rect.height;
	var logoMarginBot = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(heroLogo).marginBottom);

	// ── Constants (measured from the live DOM, not hand-synced to CSS) ──────────
	// NAV_H and navFontPx used to be literals — `64` "must match .header-inner
	// height" and `1.1rem` "matches .site-logo font-size". Both silently drift
	// the instant the stylesheet changes (the exact hand-sync hazard the
	// nav-breakpoint work kept tripping over). Reading the header's rendered
	// height and the nav logo's COMPUTED font-size ties the docking geometry to
	// whatever the CSS actually produces, at whatever the current breakpoint is.
	// Fallback literals preserve the old numbers if the elements are absent.
	var headerInner = document.querySelector('.header-inner');
	var NAV_H      = headerInner ? headerInner.getBoundingClientRect().height : 64;
	var navFontPx  = navLogo
		? parseFloat(getComputedStyle(navLogo).fontSize)
		: 1.1 * parseFloat(getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).fontSize);
	// Where the fixed logo sits so it's vertically centered in the nav bar
	var navLogoTop = (NAV_H - navFontPx) / 2;
	// The scale at which the hero logo visually matches nav-logo size
	var navScale   = navFontPx / heroFontPx;
	// Phase 1 ends when the logo's top edge has reached navLogoTop,
	// scaled by the per-device multiplier (1.0 = natural).
	var phase1End  = (startTop - navLogoTop) * cfg.phase1Multiplier;

	// (Previously defined lsStartPx / lsEndPx here for per-frame
	//  letter-spacing animation from 0.15em → 0.12em — removed
	//  2026-04-22 to eliminate text re-flow cost on mobile.
	//  See update() for the trade-off note.)


	// (Crossfade removed 2026-04-22: the hero title is now the single element
	//  that animates all the way into the nav slot, rather than two elements
	//  fading between each other at the end. See enterNav()/exitNav() and the
	//  absence of a fade-range block in update() below for the new contract.)

	// ── Insert spacer to hold the layout gap ──────────────────────────────────
	// Once heroLogo is position:fixed it leaves normal flow. Without a spacer,
	// the tagline and CTA would jump upward to fill the gap.
	var spacer = document.createElement('div');
	spacer.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
	spacer.style.height       = logoHeight    + 'px';
	spacer.style.marginBottom = logoMarginBot + 'px';
	spacer.style.flexShrink   = '0';
	heroLogo.parentNode.insertBefore(spacer, heroLogo);

	// ── Switch logo to position:fixed ─────────────────────────────────────────
	// .hero-content has no z-index (removed from CSS so it forms no stacking
	// context). The logo's z-index:200 is therefore evaluated at the root
	// stacking context and beats the nav bar's z-index:100 directly.
	heroLogo.style.position      = 'fixed';
	heroLogo.style.left          = '50%';
	heroLogo.style.top           = startTop + 'px';
	heroLogo.style.transform     = 'translateX(-50%)';
	heroLogo.style.transformOrigin = 'top center';
	heroLogo.style.zIndex        = '200';
	// pointer-events:none — prevents the invisible scaled-away hit-test box
	// from blocking middle-clicks and other events on content below
	heroLogo.style.pointerEvents = 'none';
	// No CSS transition — opacity is driven frame-by-frame from scroll position
	heroLogo.style.transition    = 'none';
	// will-change: transform — promote to a persistent compositor layer
	// before scroll starts, so the per-frame transform updates in update()
	// don't churn between layered/non-layered states. Particularly helpful
	// on mobile where layer creation has measurable cost per frame.
	heroLogo.style.willChange    = 'transform';

	// Hero logo stays fully visible throughout the animation — it's the
	// single element that slides from hero position into the nav slot.
	// Set explicitly here (not just left to CSS default) so the opacity
	// state is one-shot rather than driven per-frame.
	heroLogo.style.opacity = '1';

	// Nav logo (.site-logo) stays invisible on the home page — the hero
	// logo itself fills that visual role at the end of the animation.
	// pointer-events starts disabled and becomes enabled once the title
	// has docked into the nav slot, so clicks on the nav-logo area then
	// land on the underlying <a href="/"> .site-logo for expected
	// "click logo to go home" behavior (hero-logo is a <div>, not a link).
	if (navLogo) {
		navLogo.style.opacity       = '0';
		navLogo.style.transition    = 'none';
		navLogo.style.pointerEvents = 'none';
		// Mirror the pointer-events dance in the TAB ORDER. While the nav logo
		// is invisible (opacity 0) and non-clickable it must also be
		// unreachable by keyboard — otherwise a Tab lands on an invisible link.
		// tabIndex flips in lockstep with pointerEvents at every site below
		// (enterNav enables both, exitNav/resize disable both). This is JS-only,
		// so the no-JS path is untouched: without this script the nav logo stays
		// a normal visible, focusable <a href="/">.
		navLogo.tabIndex            = -1;
	}
	if (slotBorder) {
		// Slot border is visible from the start — it marks the nav bar's
		// bottom edge and will be visible as the title slides through it
		slotBorder.style.opacity = '1';
	}

	function lerp(a, b, t) { return a + (b - a) * t; }

	// Ease function — swappable per-device via pickConfig() above.
	// Default desktop is cubic (t*t*t) which holds near 0 for most of the
	// scroll and snaps sharply at the end. Mobile default is quadratic (t*t)
	// for a gentler finish matched to mobile's shorter scroll runway.
	// Using a var (not a function declaration) so the resize handler can
	// swap it if the viewport crosses the mobile/desktop threshold.
	var ease = cfg.easeFn;

	var inNav   = false;
	var ticking = false;

	// ── Mobile nav menu coordination ─────────────────────────────────────────
	// When the mobile hamburger menu is open, the title (position: fixed
	// during animation) overlays the menu items because they share the same
	// area at the top of the viewport. Snap the title to the docked state on
	// open so it sits in the nav slot instead. On close, restore to the
	// scroll-appropriate state. Mobile only — desktop has no hamburger.
	//
	// `navForceDocked` overrides the scroll-driven state machine: when true,
	// update() bails so it doesn't fight the snap.
	var navForceDocked = false;

	// Called once when scrollY >= phase1End — the hero title has fully
	// docked into the nav slot. Hero logo stays visible at this position
	// (no crossfade to a separate element); we just fade out the slot
	// border (the real nav bar bottom border visually takes over) and
	// enable pointer events on the underlying .site-logo so clicks on
	// the now-tiny title land on a real <a href="/">.
	//
	// SNAP TO FINAL TRANSFORM: on fast scroll (especially mobile flick-
	// scroll), the previous update() frame may have run with a scrollY
	// significantly less than phase1End — leaving the title at an
	// intermediate scale/position. The next frame fires with scrollY
	// past phase1End, enterNav() runs, inNav becomes true, and update()
	// stops touching the transform. The title visibly freezes mid-
	// animation. Setting the docked transform here guarantees the title
	// snaps to its final state regardless of how coarse the scroll
	// sampling was.
	function enterNav() {
		inNav = true;
		heroLogo.style.transform = 'translateX(-50%) translateY(' + (-phase1End) + 'px) scale(' + navScale + ')';
		if (navLogo) { navLogo.style.pointerEvents = 'auto'; navLogo.tabIndex = 0; }
		if (slotBorder) slotBorder.style.opacity = '0';
	}

	// Called when scrollY drops back below phase1End — user scrolled up
	// past the docked state, so the title resumes its scale-and-slide
	// animation. Re-enable the slot border (it'll be visible as the
	// title passes back through it) and disable the nav-logo click
	// area (the docking point is no longer underneath).
	function exitNav() {
		inNav = false;
		heroLogo.style.transformOrigin = 'top center';
		if (navLogo) { navLogo.style.pointerEvents = 'none'; navLogo.tabIndex = -1; }
		if (slotBorder) slotBorder.style.opacity = '1';
	}

	function update() {
		// If the mobile nav menu is open, the title is force-docked into the
		// nav slot. Bail before doing any scroll-driven transform work — the
		// MutationObserver below owns the title's transform until the menu
		// closes. Without this guard, the next scroll event would overwrite
		// the snap with a scroll-derived transform.
		if (navForceDocked) {
			ticking = false;
			return;
		}

		var scrollY = window.scrollY;

		if (scrollY >= phase1End) {
			if (!inNav) enterNav();
		} else {
			if (inNav) exitNav();
		}

		if (!inNav) {
			// Simulate in-flow scrolling using TRANSFORM, not `top`.
			// Why: `top` changes trigger layout (re-flow) every scroll
			// frame on mobile — the main cause of jitter on Android
			// Chrome. translateY() is composited (GPU layer), so it
			// updates without re-flow. Same visual result, smoother.
			//
			// Composition note: transforms apply right-to-left. So
			// `scale(S)` runs first (sizes the element), then
			// `translateY(Y)` shifts it in viewport pixels (Y is in
			// CSS pixels regardless of scale), then `translateX(-50%)`
			// centers horizontally based on the post-scale width.
			// Net result identical to the previous top-based approach.
			//
			// `top` itself stays at startTop (set once at setup) — no
			// per-frame layout cost.

			// p: scroll progress from 0 (page top) to 1 (title at nav position)
			var p  = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, scrollY / phase1End));
			var ep = ease(p);

			heroLogo.style.transform = 'translateX(-50%) translateY(' + (-scrollY) + 'px) scale(' + lerp(1, navScale, ep) + ')';

			// (letter-spacing animation was removed 2026-04-22 to eliminate
			//  per-frame LAYOUT work on mobile. Changing letter-spacing per
			//  frame triggers text re-flow inside the title element — the
			//  main remaining source of jitter on Android Chrome after the
			//  top→translateY refactor. Trade-off: at the docked position
			//  the title's letter-spacing is ~0.5px wider per gap than the
			//  interior pages' static nav logo (hero CSS is 0.15em, nav is
			//  0.12em). Small enough to not register visually in practice.
			//  If needed later, re-animate letter-spacing only during a
			//  specific sub-range of the scroll rather than every frame.)

			// (No opacity work needed in this block — hero logo stays fully
			//  opaque throughout the scroll, sliding under the nav slot border
			//  which has z-index 201 while the logo has z-index 200.)

			// ── Scroll hint ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
			// Stays fully visible until 35% scroll, then dissolves by 65%.
			// Slower fade feels more considered than the immediate flash-out.
			if (scrollHint) {
				var hp = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (p - 0.35) / 0.30));
				scrollHint.style.opacity = String(1 - hp);
			}
		}

		ticking = false;
	}

	window.addEventListener('scroll', function () {
		if (!ticking) { requestAnimationFrame(update); ticking = true; }
	}, { passive: true });

	// Unified resize handler — recalculates everything position-related on
	// any viewport change, and re-picks cfg in case the user crossed the
	// mobile/desktop threshold (e.g., device rotation on a tablet). The
	// earlier "if mobile, tear down state" branch was removed when we
	// enabled the animation on mobile — both form factors now run it.
	//
	// Width-only gate: mobile browsers fire resize events constantly during
	// the address-bar dance (Firefox Android especially), where only the
	// height changes. Running the full recalc + transform reset on those
	// events caused a one-frame flash where the title rendered at scale 1
	// without translateX(-50%) — visible as "title becomes huge and shoved
	// right" on slow scroll-up. Skipping height-only resizes eliminates the
	// flash entirely; address-bar dance only changes height, never width,
	// so this is safe for that case while still catching real resizes
	// (orientation change, window resize, devtools open) which all do
	// change width.
	var lastResizeWidth = window.innerWidth;
	window.addEventListener('resize', function () {
		if (window.innerWidth === lastResizeWidth) {
			// Height-only change (mobile address-bar dance). Just re-run
			// update() to re-apply the current scroll position transform —
			// no measurement work, no transform clear, no flash.
			if (!ticking) { requestAnimationFrame(update); ticking = true; }
			return;
		}
		lastResizeWidth = window.innerWidth;

		// Re-pick per-device config. If the threshold was crossed, `cfg`
		// swaps; otherwise this is a cheap no-op.
		cfg  = pickConfig();
		ease = cfg.easeFn;

		// Read fontSize directly — getComputedStyle returns the COMPUTED
		// CSS value, which is independent of any transforms applied to
		// the element. The previous `heroLogo.style.transform = ''` clear
		// was unnecessary AND was the source of the address-bar-flash bug
		// (one frame of cleared transform = title at scale 1, no centering).
		heroFontPx = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(heroLogo).fontSize);

		// Spacer's bounding rect tracks the logo's intended document position
		// across viewport width changes — this is why we inserted the spacer.
		var spRect = spacer.getBoundingClientRect();
		startTop   = spRect.top + window.scrollY;
		navScale   = navFontPx / heroFontPx;
		phase1End  = (startTop - navLogoTop) * cfg.phase1Multiplier;
		// (letter-spacing recomputation removed — animation no longer runs)

		// update() now uses transform: translateY(-scrollY) for scroll
		// tracking instead of style.top, so we need to update the base
		// `top` here when startTop changes (orientation change, real
		// viewport resize). Final viewport position = top + translateY
		// = startTop + (-scrollY) = startTop - scrollY, which matches
		// the previous top-based formula exactly.
		heroLogo.style.top = startTop + 'px';

		inNav = false;
		heroLogo.style.opacity         = '1';
		heroLogo.style.transformOrigin = 'top center';
		if (navLogo) { navLogo.style.opacity = '0'; navLogo.style.pointerEvents = 'none'; navLogo.tabIndex = -1; }
		if (slotBorder) slotBorder.style.opacity = '1';

		// Menu open during the resize (rotate with hamburger menu showing):
		// re-snap to the dock position computed from the FRESH measurements
		// above. Without this, the stale pre-rotate translateY stayed applied
		// — update() bails while navForceDocked — so the docked title jumped
		// by the startTop delta until the menu closed (QA issue #93, item 4).
		// Inlined rather than calling snapToDocked() (declared in a block
		// further down; block-level function hoisting is sloppy-mode-only
		// semantics not worth depending on). No transition here: mid-resize,
		// instant placement is what you want.
		if (navForceDocked) {
			heroLogo.style.transform =
				'translateX(-50%) translateY(' + (-phase1End) + 'px) scale(' + navScale + ')';
		}

		if (!ticking) { requestAnimationFrame(update); ticking = true; }
	}, { passive: true });

	// ── Mobile nav menu observer ────────────────────────────────────────────
	// Watch the site header for `.nav-open` class changes. The class is
	// toggled by the hamburger handler in base.njk (also by the
	// click-outside-to-close handler), so a MutationObserver is more reliable
	// than wiring into either entry point. Mobile only — desktop has no
	// hamburger so the class never toggles there, but check explicitly in
	// case the breakpoint behavior changes.
	var siteHeaderEl = document.getElementById('site-header');
	if (siteHeaderEl) {

		// Apply the docked-state transform (same shape as enterNav, but with
		// a brief CSS transition so the snap is smooth — the title may be at
		// full size when the user taps the hamburger).
		function snapToDocked() {
			heroLogo.style.transition = 'transform 0.2s ease-out';
			heroLogo.style.transform =
				'translateX(-50%) translateY(' + (-phase1End) + 'px) scale(' + navScale + ')';
		}

		// Compute and apply the transform that matches the current scroll
		// position — used when the menu closes. Mirrors the math inside
		// update()'s !inNav block. We don't just call update() here because
		// update() bails when navForceDocked is true (it's still true at
		// this moment — we clear the flag below after the transition starts).
		function restoreFromForceDocked() {
			heroLogo.style.transition = 'transform 0.2s ease-out';
			var scrollY = window.scrollY;
			if (scrollY >= phase1End) {
				heroLogo.style.transform =
					'translateX(-50%) translateY(' + (-phase1End) + 'px) scale(' + navScale + ')';
			} else {
				var p  = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, scrollY / phase1End));
				var ep = ease(p);
				heroLogo.style.transform =
					'translateX(-50%) translateY(' + (-scrollY) + 'px) scale(' + lerp(1, navScale, ep) + ')';
			}
		}

		// Transition-strip timer handle — stored so rapid open→close toggles
		// can cancel the pending strip. Without the clearTimeout below, an
		// open immediately followed by a close (<250ms) let the FIRST timer
		// fire mid-restore and strip the transition, teleporting the title
		// partway through its 200ms animation (QA issue #93, item 3).
		var transitionStripTimer = null;

		new MutationObserver(function () {
			var isOpen = siteHeaderEl.classList.contains('nav-open');
			if (isOpen && !navForceDocked) {
				// Width-gate ONLY the snap/open branch. The restore branch is
				// deliberately ungated: if the viewport crosses the collapse
				// breakpoint while the menu is open, the eventual close must
				// still be processed — gating both directions left
				// navForceDocked stuck true forever (update() bailing on every
				// scroll frame, title frozen in the nav until reload). That
				// was QA issue #93. Ungated restore also means recovery never
				// depends on the manually-synced breakpoint constant.
				if (window.innerWidth > NAV_COLLAPSE_MAX) return;
				navForceDocked = true;
				snapToDocked();
			} else if (!isOpen && navForceDocked) {
				navForceDocked = false;
				restoreFromForceDocked();
			} else {
				return; // No state change, skip the transition cleanup below.
			}

			// Strip the CSS transition shortly after the snap completes so
			// subsequent scroll-driven transform writes apply instantly
			// (with a lingering transition, every scroll frame would animate
			// over 200ms and the scroll tracking would lag visibly).
			if (transitionStripTimer) clearTimeout(transitionStripTimer);
			transitionStripTimer = setTimeout(function () { heroLogo.style.transition = 'none'; }, 250);
		}).observe(siteHeaderEl, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ['class'] });
	}

	// Run once on load in case the page restores to a scrolled position
	update();

	// bfcache (back/forward cache) restores resurrect the page with whatever
	// transform state was frozen at navigate-away time — the title can come
	// back stuck mid-slot. `pageshow` with e.persisted === true fires only on
	// those restores. update() is idempotent (reads scrollY and reapplies the
	// transform from scratch), so re-running it here resyncs the title to the
	// actual restored scroll position.
	window.addEventListener('pageshow', function (e) {
		if (e.persisted) update();
	});
}());
</script>
